# What this hides, and why.
#
# nginx 1.30.6 calls ngx_memcpy(dst, NULL, 0) from ngx_pstrdup while
# ngx_init_cycle copies the prefix strings -- before a single module is
# loaded.  memcpy is declared __attribute__((nonnull)), so a null source
# is undefined behaviour even when the length is zero and nothing is read
# or written.  The stack is nginx's own all the way down, measured here:
#
#     #0 ngx_pstrdup    src/core/ngx_string.c:84
#     #1 ngx_init_cycle src/core/ngx_cycle.c:118
#     #2 main           src/core/nginx.c:293
#
# This module appears in none of those reports.  The entry comes out again
# as soon as the shipped stable line no longer carries it.
#
# This is the only entry, and that is measured, not assumed: with an empty
# file the suite reports exactly this and nothing else.  A suppression
# written on the chance that it might be needed hides whatever lands on
# that name later, including something of ours.
#
# The file also has to exist at all: UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer treats a
# suppressions path it cannot read as fatal, and nginx then does not start
# under the sanitizer, which fails the job for a reason that has nothing
# to do with the code.
nonnull-attribute:ngx_pstrdup
